# tools/ales_cue_seal_witness.rish — the cue sheet, sealed (ALES37).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES37.
# Design read: active-designing/20260814-153033_fill-ales37-lotus-cue-seal.md
# Stands on: lotus/cue_sheet.rye (ALES22) — render_sheet / parse_sheet reused whole as the sealed body; lotus/markers.rye (ALES20) —
# the Markers track sealed and rebuilt. Mirrors lotus/rack_seal.rye (ALES34) and the Sha256 verify-before-trust of lotus/wire.rye (ALES0).
#
# lotus/cue_seal.rye seals an ALES22 cue sheet with a Sha256 content digest and refuses to parse a record whose digest does not match
# its body — the wire's verify-before-trust (and the ALES34 rack seal) handed to the saveable arrangement map. The crux: a sealed cue
# sheet parses back to exactly the same markers track ONLY when its digest matches its body — seal_sheet renders the plain ALES22
# record as the body and writes a Sha256 over exactly those bytes, and verify_and_parse recomputes the digest and hands the body to
# cue_sheet.parse_sheet only on a match (which then bounds each position against the master), so a hand-edited position, a truncated
# tail, or a changed name is caught at the gate before a single marker is trusted. The selftest proves: a sealed four-marker track
# round-trips whole; a flipped body byte and a truncated tail refuse DigestMismatch; a plain record, a missing, short, or non-hex digest
# line refuses BadRecord; a seal into a too-small buffer refuses Overflow. The seal proves a sheet arrived whole, not who wrote it — no
# signature, no keys, no network, no funds.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_cue_seal_witness.rish

say "ales-cue-seal: Language EN — ALES37 gives the ALES22 cue sheet a Sha256 content digest, so a keeper's saved arrangement map verifies before it is trusted: a hand-edited marker position, a truncated file, a changed name is caught at the gate rather than parsed as some other arrangement — the same verify-before-trust the ALES34 rack seal keeps for the held audio, revealing the seal as a law for any travels-as-text record."
say "ales-cue-seal: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively: a sealed cue sheet parses back to exactly the same markers track ONLY when its digest matches its body — seal_sheet wraps ALES22 whole and hashes exactly the rendered body, and verify_and_parse checks the digest BEFORE trust and hands the body to cue_sheet.parse_sheet only on a match, so no corrupt record ever reaches the parser."
say "ales-cue-seal: Lens TAME — cue_sheet.render_sheet / parse_sheet reused whole (the seal adds only the digest layer, no new grammar); the one honest difference from the rack seal is that parse_sheet bounds positions against a master, so verify_and_parse passes the master through; a malformed header or digest line refuses BadRecord (structural), a body that no longer matches its digest refuses DigestMismatch (content), each before a marker is parsed; siloed to lotus/, no signature, no key, no network, no funds, no device, no meter, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/cue_seal"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/cue_seal.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-cue-seal: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/cue_seal.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-cue-seal: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-cue-seal: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-cue-seal' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-cue-seal: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-cue-seal line"

say "ales-cue-seal: GREEN — a keeper's saved arrangement map verifies before it is trusted: seal_sheet writes a Sha256 over exactly the ALES22 body, verify_and_parse refuses DigestMismatch on any edited position or truncated tail before a single marker is parsed and BadRecord on a malformed seal, a sealed four-marker track round-trips whole, and a too-small buffer refuses Overflow. With both the rack sheet (ALES34) and the cue sheet sealed, verify-before-trust is shown to be general; the next purely-local rung is a session file bundling the rack and the cue sheet over one master. The SIGNED carry (via a Kumara key) and the ALES0-wire carry stay module seams for Keaton's word."
